WEBVTT

NOTE week08-clip3-irregular-signals
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.646 line:2 align:center
In the ICU, measurements
arrive at irregular times.

2
00:00:03.646 --> 00:00:07.850 line:2 align:center
Heart rate is charted about every
hour, with a long gap overnight,

3
00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:12.350 line:2 align:center
and lactate is drawn only
three times in the whole day.

4
00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:16.087 line:2 align:center
The first option is to put
everything on an hourly grid.

5
00:00:16.087 --> 00:00:20.226 line:2 align:center
Empty hours are filled with
the last measured value,

6
00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:26.083 line:2 align:center
and a mask of ones and zeros records
which values were actually measured.

7
00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:30.136 line:2 align:center
The mask matters, because
missing values carry information.

8
00:00:30.136 --> 00:00:34.353 line:2 align:center
Nobody draws a lactate at two
in the morning without a reason.

9
00:00:34.353 --> 00:00:40.283 line:2 align:center
The mask lets the model use when a
test was ordered, not just its result.

10
00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:45.408 line:2 align:center
The second option is the one from clip
one: interval tokens between events.

11
00:00:45.408 --> 00:00:50.780 line:2 align:center
The overnight gap is no longer
missing data. It becomes a token,

12
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.350 line:2 align:center
two to six hours, that the model can read.

13
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.989 line:2 align:center
The third option is a time embedding,

14
00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:03.366 line:2 align:center
like the sine and cosine position waves
from week two, but measured in real hours.

15
00:01:03.366 --> 00:01:11.039 line:2 align:center
At two and a half hours, the eight-hour
wave gives 0.92 and minus 0.38,

16
00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:17.750 line:2 align:center
and the 24-hour wave gives 0.61 and 0.79.

17
00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:23.204 line:2 align:center
Dense signals such as the ECG are cut
into patches, exactly as in week six.

18
00:01:23.204 --> 00:01:29.140 line:2 align:center
A ten-second ECG at 500 samples per
second becomes 10 one-second patches

19
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.150 line:2 align:center
of 500 samples each.

20
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:37.344 line:2 align:center
So every clinical data type can
become tokens: codes, lab values,

21
00:01:37.344 --> 00:01:39.917 line:2 align:center
time, missing values, and signals. Each
choice decides what the model can see,

22
00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:45.547 line:2 align:center
and what it cannot.
